  "case": {
    "presentation": "A 67-year-old man with a history of hypothyroidism presents with daily palpitations and increased fatigue. Last year, a Ziopatch showed atrial fibrillation for 1 minute and 15 seconds. On exam today in the clinic, he is in normal sinus rhythm. I have referred him to Cardiology. Should I start this patient on a DOAC in anticipation for possible ablation?",
    "recommendations": "Please obtain an echocardiogram, an ECG, and a stress test, and order thyroid function tests given his hypothyroidism history. The stress test would be useful for consideration of class IC anti-arrhythmic drugs for symptomatic atrial fibrillation. Assuming he has no hypertension, heart failure, or diabetes, his CHA2DS2-VASc score is 1, and so a DOAC is not mandatory unless his score increases. If his AF becomes more persistent and he needs DC cardioversion, then he would need to be on a DOAC for that."
